  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nGOES infrared satellite imagery revels extensive cloud cover \nspreading across Upper Michigan early this morning, associated with \na plume of rich mid level moisture which extends northeastward into \nthe Great Lakes from a deep trough and surface low lifting into the \nsouthern Plains. Precipitation associated with this system remains \nwell south of the UP this morning over Wisconsin and Lower Michigan. \nMeanwhile, another deep low pressure system is located well to the \nnorth over Hudson Bay, with a cold front extending southwestward. \nSurface analysis places this feature on the doorstep of Lake \nSuperior and the western UP as of 3 AM EDT Thursday. Ahead of the \nfront, overnight temperatures are quite warm across the area given a \npersistent weak warm advection regime and insulating cloud cover, \nwith most locations remaining in the upper 50s to low 60s as of this \nwriting.\n\nThe surface cold front will sweep across the UP later this morning \nand bring a quick shift to breezy west-northwest winds in its wake \nthis afternoon. Daytime highs will struggle to get out of the mid \n60s across most of the area today as 850 mb temps plummet from 12-13 \nC this morning to around 5 C by this afternoon. Can't rule out a few \nrain showers towards Menominee County and the northern shores of \nLake Michigan today, but expect dry weather across most of the UP as \nprecipitation associated with the southern stream system remains \nsouth of the area. Could see some limited fire weather concerns \ndevelop across interior locations this afternoon as dewpoints mix \nout efficiently and yield RH values in the low 30s as winds gust to \n15-20 mph. Even breezier conditions are likely near Lake Superior \nwhere guidance still shows a high probability of wind gusts of 30+ \nmph from the Keweenaw eastward along the shoreline. \n\nTemperatures will be markedly colder tonight as cold advection takes \nhold and skies clear behind the departing front. While continued \nwindy conditions will likely keep lows from truly dropping off the \ntable, still expect widespread temps in the 30s away from Lake \nSuperior, potentially reaching freezing in a few of the colder spots \nin the interior. Breezy conditions continue into Friday as CAA \nregime continues and high pressure building in from the west \nprovides an isallobaric push. Will likely maintain limited fire \nweather concerns into the afternoon as RH values once again drop to \naround 30%, though temperatures will be a bit cooler in the 50s. \nWill also need to consider some lake effect rain showers in the east \nas 850 mb temps drop to around -5 C while lake surface temperatures \ncontinue to reside around 13-16 C. Temperatures really drop off on \nFriday night as high pressure moves directly overhead and brings an \nend to the breezy conditions, allowing for optimal radiational \ncooling. Could see a hard freeze for many interior locations as \ntemperatures drop into the 20s, with areas near the lakeshores \nlikely remaining in the mid to upper 30s. Highs rebound slightly \ninto the low 60s during the day on Saturday. A low passing north of \nLake Superior then brings another chance for widespread rain late \nSaturday into Sunday morning, but rain amounts look to be rather \nunimpressive again as NBM probs for even a tenth of an inch only \nsite at 25-35% across most of the area, higher in the far east. High \npressure then returns for the early part of next week.",
